  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q19-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "19",
    "prompt": "An airline is considering which routes to fly between a collection of $n$ cities. How many different choices does the airline have for their total offering of flights? Include the trivial case, where the airline offers no flights at all. Treat a route between two cities as one choice, regardless of direction.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2^{\\binom{n}{2}}$ choices.\n\nChoose any subset of the possible routes between pairs of cities.",
      "worked": "There are $\\binom{n}{2}$ possible routes, one for each unordered pair of cities. Each route is either included or excluded independently. By the multiplication principle,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Number of offerings}&=2^{\\binom{n}{2}}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe empty subset represents offering no flights.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
